  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 PM EDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nCurrently, a shortwave/low pressure system is moving into the \nnorthern Great Plains with a near 5,950m ridge centered to our west. \nSurface high pressure remains largely in place across the eastern \nU.S. This will lead to another hot and largely dry day across the \nregion. With PWAT values sitting around 1.3 to 1.5 inches, moisture \nis sufficient for isolated showers or storms along the higher \nterrain, but most places will stay dry. By Monday, the upper high \nwill strengthen and shift slightly east, leading to a further \nincrease in temperatures into the 90s. Isolated rain chances \ncontinue along the higher terrain, but this will remain limited. By \nTuesday and Wednesday, the 500mb high will remain near 5,950m and \ncentered directly over the region, promoting widespread 90-degree \ntemperatures. With similar moisture in place, heat index values will \nreach or exceed 100 degrees across most of the area, potentially 105 \ndegrees in the southern Valley. The upper high begins to shift \nfurther south on Thursday, with an increase in moisture leading to a \nreturn of rain chances. \n\nFriday and Saturday, a closed low will move into New England, \nleading to further suppression of high pressure and northwesterly \nflow aloft. With sufficient moisture in place, chances for diurnal \nshowers and storms will increase above Thursday, also leading to \nsome reduction in temperatures as well. With heating and moisture, a \nfew stronger storms are possible, but we are certainly in a \nclimatologically less favorable time of year.",
